  "problem": "Consider the equation ${y}^{\u2033}+12{y}^{\u2032}+40y=0,$ where $y=y(x).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$",
  "worked_solution": "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{\u2033}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+12\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+40{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +40) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +40=0\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +40 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +36)-36+40 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +6 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-6\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lambda =-6\\pm 2\\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation.",
